N('irondale-schoolhouse-millerton','Irondale Schoolhouse','Millerton','hud','civic','standing',41.953,-73.5114,'site','open','walk','','1858',
  'A one-room schoolhouse of 1858 from Irondale, the iron-making settlement just north of Millerton where the Millerton Iron Company ran its furnace. It stood beside NY 22 until 2013, when it was moved into the village to stand at the Main Street entrance to the Harlem Valley Rail Trail as a schoolroom museum and visitors\' centre. The Irondale Schoolhouse Association opens it each summer.',
  'Slate chalkboards children can still write on, a school bell rung by a rope, and a room set out as a 19th-century school (The Millerton News). The Pomeroy marker that tells of the move stands at the old site on NY 22.',
  'A one-room schoolhouse moved to the rail trail.',
  'Run by the Irondale Schoolhouse Association at the Harlem Valley Rail Trail entrance on Main Street. Hours are from The Millerton News\'s report on the 2025 season, not from the association\'s own page, which was not found. Village parking lot just north on Main Street.',
  [L('The Millerton News: Irondale Schoolhouse rings bell on 11th season','https://millertonnews.com/irondale-schoolhouse-rings-bell-on-11th-season-ice-cream-social-june-8'),L('The Millerton News: historic Irondale Schoolhouse to open for the season (2021)','https://millertonnews.com/millerton/historic-irondale-schoolhouse-open-season-may-29'),HM(132682,'Irondale School'),W('Millerton, New York')],
  th=[41.95363,-73.51156],thn='Village lot on Main Street',brick='?',hours='Summer: Saturday and Sunday 11 a.m. to 2 p.m. (2025 season)'),
N('moviehouse-millerton','The Moviehouse (old Millerton Grange hall)','Millerton','hud','civic','standing',41.95353,-73.50993,'exact','open','walk','','1905',
  'Built in 1905 as a Grange hall, with films shown in an upstairs room from about 1915. An opera house is listed in Millerton in 1914–15, and by 1927 it was the Millerton Theatre, with 360 seats. It is now a four-screen cinema called The Moviehouse, on the main street of Millerton\'s Register historic district (65 contributing buildings, listed 2010).',
  'A working cinema in the old hall. What survives of the 1905 interior was not checked. The rest of Main Street is the village\'s historic commercial core.',
  'A 1905 Grange hall that has shown films for a century.',
  'Working cinema at 48 Main Street (OpenStreetMap). Showtimes change; check before you go. Village parking lots off Main Street.',
  [L('Cinema Treasures: Moviehouse, Millerton','https://cinematreasures.org/theaters/18351'),W('Main Street Historic District (Millerton, New York)'),W('Millerton, New York')],
  th=[41.95345,-73.51197],thn='Village lot, North Center Street',brick='?',hours='?'),
N('ezra-clark-house-coleman-station','Ezra Clark House','Coleman Station','hud','landmark','standing',41.92056,-73.51778,'site','view','walk','','1780',
  'One of the oldest farmhouses in the town of North East and one of the few of brick, at the centre of the Coleman Station farm district. It was long said to be Ezra Clark\'s and the oldest house round Millerton, but a chimney brick carved "J.M. 1780" suggests a later date and another builder. The district round it, nine farms first settled by New Englanders, later sold milk to New York City from the Harlem line\'s Coleman station.',
  'A two-story, five-bay brick house over a stone basement, with a slate roof in coloured patterns, two brick chimneys and a two-tier front porch. The Register text describes a kitchen fireplace with a Dutch beehive oven inside. The old railway behind the house is now the Harlem Valley Rail Trail.',
  '',
  'Private house on Mill Road just north of Indian Lake Road. See it from Mill Road or from the Harlem Valley Rail Trail along its back line; do not go onto the lot. No mapped parking nearby.',
  [W('Ezra Clark House'),W('Coleman Station Historic District')],
  brick='yes'),
N('beth-david-synagogue-amenia','Beth David Synagogue','Amenia','hud','worship','standing',41.85045,-73.55252,'site','view','drive','','late 1920s',
  'Built in the late 1920s by Amenia\'s small community of Russian Jewish emigrants. The congregation began Orthodox and moved to Conservative and then Reform practice as it shrank. Listed on the Register in 2002, the only synagogue east of the Hudson and north of New York City then listed.',
  'A small one-story building faced in brick veneer over hollow tile with cast-stone trim and tall round-arched windows. Wikipedia describes an Orthodox layout inside: a central bimah ringed by theatre-style upholstered pews of dark-stained pine, facing a Torah ark painted with candlesticks and scrolls and topped by gilt Lions of Judah. Whether visitors can go in was not checked.',
  'A 1920s country synagogue with its painted ark.',
  'Active congregation at 3344 East Main Street (NY 343), about a quarter-mile east of the crossroads. No page of its own giving services was found. See it from the sidewalk; the Harlem Valley Rail Trail lot is a short walk south.',
  [W('Beth David Synagogue (Amenia, New York)')],
  th=[41.8467,-73.55146],thn='Harlem Valley Rail Trail lot, Amenia',brick='yes',hours='?'),
N('indian-rock-schoolhouse-amenia','Indian Rock Schoolhouse','Amenia','hud','civic','standing',41.86111,-73.54611,'site','view','drive','','mid-1800s–1927',
  'A one-room schoolhouse built in the mid-19th century to New York\'s standard plan for rural schools, with separate entrances for boys and girls. Of twelve such schools in the town of Amenia it is the only one left mostly intact. It closed in 1927; the local historical society\'s president gave it to the Webutuck Schoolhouse Association of the neighbouring Maplebrook School, which restored it by 2004 for community events and history lessons.',
  'A board-and-batten school with a steep cedar-shingled roof, 40 feet back from Mygatt Road. Inside, original low wainscoting with carved initials, some original plaster and blackboards, and channels in the floor where desks were bolted down (Wikipedia). It is used for events; no open hours were found. Amenia Rural Cemetery is across the curve of the road.',
  '',
  'Owned by the Webutuck Schoolhouse Association (Maplebrook School). No open hours found; see it from Mygatt Road. Pull off only where it is safe.',
  [W('Indian Rock Schoolhouse')],
  brick='no'),
N('troutbeck-amenia','Troutbeck','Amenia','hud','hotel','standing',41.8546,-73.51676,'site','open','drive','','1919',
  'An estate on the Webutuck River, home of the poet-naturalist Myron Benton, friend of John Burroughs and correspondent of Thoreau. Joel and Amy Spingarn bought it in the early 1900s and held two early NAACP gatherings there, the Amenia Conferences of 1916 and 1933, attended by W.E.B. Du Bois and Mary White Ovington. The old house burned on December 16, 1917, and the Spingarns built the present stone manor house by 1919. It is now a hotel and restaurant.',
  'The 1919 stone manor house and Joel Spingarn\'s walled garden of 1916, where he grew over 250 kinds of clematis (Troutbeck\'s own history page). A roadside marker (Troutbeck) stands on NY 343. Interior beyond the public rooms not checked.',
  'Where the NAACP\'s early leaders met in 1916 and 1933.',
  'Private hotel at 515 Leedsville Road (OpenStreetMap). Whether non-guests may visit the grounds or dine was not checked; its hours were not read.',
  [L('Troutbeck: a brief history','https://troutbeck.com/discover/about/a-brief-history/'),HM(144291,'Troutbeck')],
  brick='no',hours='?'),
N('maxon-mills-wassaic','Maxon Mills grain elevator','Wassaic','hud','industry','standing',41.8058,-73.56051,'exact','open','drive','','1950s',
  'A seven-story wood-crib grain elevator built in the 1950s beside the Harlem line in Wassaic, a hamlet that had grown round the Reed, Gridley and Co. iron works (in business by 1829) and the railroad. The mill employed people into the 1980s. It was about to be torn down in 2005 when two developers restored it; since 2008 it has been the gallery of the Wassaic Project, an artists\' group that also uses the 1875 Luther livestock barn nearby.',
  'The tall elevator itself, now seven floors of exhibition space; only the ground floor is heated and there is no lift above it. Public art stands round the hamlet.',
  'A 1950s grain elevator turned into a seven-floor gallery.',
  'Wassaic Project, 37 Furnace Bank Road. Suggested donation $10. Free parking at the mill. Hours change through the year: weekends during the summer and winter exhibitions and at events, otherwise closed (its visit page, read November 2026). Wassaic station on Metro-North is a mile north.',
  [L('Wassaic Project: visit','https://www.wassaicproject.org/visit'),L('Wassaic Project: the hamlet of Wassaic','https://www.wassaicproject.org/about/the-hamlet-of-wassaic'),W('The Wassaic Project'),HM(138022,'Wassaic Iron')],
  brick='?',hours='Weekends during the summer and winter exhibitions, and at events; Art Nest Saturdays noon to 5 p.m.'),
N('smithfield-presbyterian-church','Smithfield Presbyterian Church','Amenia','hud','worship','standing',41.8861,-73.6001,'exact','view','drive','','1847–1848',
  'The religious society here was founded in 1742 and still meets once a year, on the last Sunday in January (The Millbrook Independent). The present Greek Revival church was built in 1847–48 and refurbished in 1938; George Whitefield preached an open-air revival sermon at the earlier church in June 1770. The churchyard\'s oldest stone is dated 1737.',
  'A one-story clapboarded timber-frame church on a stone foundation, with a portico of four Ionic columns. A horse shed, the sexton\'s house of 1857 and the old cemetery stand with it. The church holds a concert series and a Christmas concert; interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Active church at 656 Smithfield Valley Road (Town of Amenia list of houses of worship). No service times were found on a page of its own. The cemetery can be seen from the road.',
  [W('Smithfield Presbyterian Church (Amenia, New York)'),L('The Millbrook Independent: a short history of Smithfield Church','https://themillbrookindependent.com/a-short-history-of-smithfield-church/'),L('Town of Amenia: houses of worship','https://ameniany.gov/houses-of-worship')],
  brick='no'),
N('st-thomas-church-amenia-union','St. Thomas\' Church, Amenia Union','Amenia Union','hud','worship','standing',41.82505,-73.50593,'exact','open','drive','','1849–1851',
  'One of a series of small rural parish churches Richard Upjohn, architect of Trinity Church in New York, designed between 1845 and 1850, and counted by the parish and the books it cites among the finest American versions of the English Gothic country church. It was built in 1849–51 under its vicar Homer Wheaton.',
  'Walls of locally made brick, with white marble water table and buttress caps; the brick was painted white in the early 1950s, so the contrast is gone. Three lancet windows at the east end stand for the Trinity. Plain inside by Upjohn\'s own rule against needless ornament; interior details not checked.',
  'An Upjohn country church in the hills by the Connecticut line.',
  'Active Episcopal parish at 40 Leedsville Road. Service time from the parish\'s own site and the Episcopal Asset Map; services are in English and Spanish.',
  [L('St. Thomas\' Church: history','http://www.stthomasamenia.com/history'),L('St. Thomas\' Church','http://www.stthomasamenia.com'),L('Episcopal Asset Map: St. Thomas\', Amenia Union','https://www.episcopalassetmap.org/node/356')],
  brick='no',hours='Sunday 10:30 a.m.'),
N('nine-partners-meeting-house','Nine Partners Meeting House and Cemetery','Millbrook','midh','worship','standing',41.77577,-73.68752,'exact','view','drive','','1780',
  'Friends from Cape Cod, Nantucket and Rhode Island built this brick meeting house in 1780, the third on the site and at twice its budget. It was the largest Quaker meeting in the Hudson Valley, and through the Civil War the meeting and its school coordinated Underground Railroad work in Dutchess and Columbia counties. After the 1826 split the Hicksites kept it; in 1897 it passed to the Nine Partners Burial Ground Association.',
  'A two-and-a-half-story brick building with arched brick lintels and end chimneys, nearly unaltered. Inside, per Wikipedia, one room divided by sliding wooden partitions on both floors to part men and women, rows of wooden benches and a raised facing bench, a gallery above, horsehair plaster and unpainted panelling. The cemetery fills the north and west of the 9-acre lot; a fence with brick gates runs along NY 343. Still used for occasional Quaker events; interior not checked.',
  'A 1780 brick Quaker meeting house with its sliding partitions intact.',
  'At the corner of NY 343 and Church Street. The cemetery is run by Lyall Memorial Federated Church and is still in use. No open days were found; see the building from the road and the cemetery gates.',
  [W('Nine Partners Meeting House and Cemetery')],
  brick='yes'),
